codex
1. Performance hypothesis: the `event-fast-par` host loop was paying avoidable overhead on every substep/flush round by `flatMap`-ing emitted messages, allocating fresh bucket arrays, and rescanning all partitions to find touched targets. Reusing buckets and recording touched partitions during bucketing should reduce host-side allocation and GC pressure in the benchmark hot path.

2. What changed: in [engine-fast-par-host.js](C:\Users\suzuk\AppData\Local\Temp\fact-sim-auto-patch\1773932058956-11796\js\app\engine-fast-par-host.js), I replaced the old `bucketMessages(...)` flow with `createMessageBuckets(...)` + `rebucketPartitionMessages(...)`. `_stepParallel()` now rebuckets directly from worker result rows, reuses the same bucket arrays across flush rounds, and only flushes partitions that were actually touched. No worker logic, partitioning logic, `dt`, or `event` behavior was modified.

3. Focused verification run: `node --check` passed for [engine-fast-par-host.js](C:\Users\suzuk\AppData\Local\Temp\fact-sim-auto-patch\1773932058956-11796\js\app\engine-fast-par-host.js), [engine-fast-par-worker.js](C:\Users\suzuk\AppData\Local\Temp\fact-sim-auto-patch\1773932058956-11796\js\app\engine-fast-par-worker.js), and [engine-fast-par-partitioner.js](C:\Users\suzuk\AppData\Local\Temp\fact-sim-auto-patch\1773932058956-11796\js\app\engine-fast-par-partitioner.js). I did not run full `quick`/`standard` parity or benchmark locally in this trimmed patch workspace.

4. Residual risk: correctness risk is low because message contents, partition targets, and flush ordering are unchanged, but the speedup may be modest if `parallel_benchmark` is dominated by worker-side execution rather than host coordination. The runner’s strict final parity and benchmark pass should confirm whether this clears the 1% keep threshold.
